# Serving with Accountability

Christian leadership is a calling to serve, not a license to act without counsel. Healthy ministry makes room for trusted relationships, clear responsibilities, and honest review.

## Make room for shared wisdom

Leaders benefit from colleagues who can ask questions, offer perspective, and speak candidly. Regular conversations with a board, ministry team, mentor, or peer group can bring blind spots into view before they become harmful patterns. Seeking counsel is a sign of care for the people entrusted to a ministry.

## Handle responsibilities transparently

Clear policies for decisions, finances, communication, and pastoral care protect both leaders and the community. Keep appropriate records, explain decisions to the people affected, and establish more than one person’s oversight for important matters. Simple, consistent practices build confidence over time.

## Receive correction as part of service

No leader sees every situation perfectly. A willingness to listen, make amends, and change course can strengthen a ministry’s witness. Accountability is not about suspicion; it is a practical expression of humility, stewardship, and love for the church.
